    89                          ; About this document
    90                          ;
    91                          ; See mos.png
    92                          ;
    93                          ; ***************************************************************************************
    94                          ;
    95                          ; This is a fully annotated reassembly of the operating system of the BBC Micro, v1.20.
    96                          ;
    97                          ; It attempts to explain every corner of the 16K ROM in some detail, hopefully throwing light
    98                          ; onto often poorly understood sections, and in general to admire its vision, scope, ingenuity
    99                          ; and succinctness. It also documents some of its few quirks / bugs. By creating this document
   100                          ; I understand it a lot better now, and I hope that readers will gain some insight too.
   101                          ;
   102                          ; In the comments I often refer to 'NAUG' the 'New Advanced User Guide'
   103                          ; see https://stardot.org.uk/forums/viewtopic.php?f=42&t=17243.
   104                          ;
   105                          ; This document was originally based on the annotated disassembly by Geoff Cox (Micronet,
   106                          ; 1991) for which I am indebted. It has evolved considerably since it started development
   107                          ; in late 2019, and development has continued well into 2020.
   108                          ;
   109                          ; Just as the BBC Micro hardware contained both innovation and also points of similarity
   110                          ; with Acorn's System 2 to 5 and Atom microcomputers, so too the MOS operating system
   111                          ; design shows clear signs of having evolved from their "monitors". As noted above, the
   112                          ; Atom monitor occupied just 4K, and the System 2 was even smaller, at 2K: nevertheless
   113                          ; they contributed design ideas to the MOS, and occasional "Historical Notes" below give
   114                          ; details. The tiny System 1, a control keypad now best known for having appeared in an
   115                          ; episode of "Blake's 7", had only 512 bytes of ROM and is not really in the same family.
   116                          ;
   117                          ; Thanks to Graham Nelson whose Inweb software is used to format/layout the HTML version of
   118                          ; this document, and for his helpful comments, corrections and suggestions and to Adrian
   119                          ; Nelson for more corrections and suggestions.
   120                          ;
   121                          ; tobymnelson@gmail.com
   122                          
   123                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   124                          ;
   125                          ; Building with the ACME Assembler
   126                          ;
   127                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   128                          ;
   129                          ; This document is downloadable ('os120_acme.a') in the syntax for the ACME assembler (as
   130                          ; well as being browsable in HTML format).
   131                          ;
   132                          ; To assemble, producing os120.bin as a binary, and a report file os120_report.txt:
   133                          ;
   134                          ;       acme -r os120_report.txt -o os120.bin os120_acme.a
   135                          ;
   136                          ; When assembled it should produce a binary file that is byte for byte identical to the
   137                          ; original OS1.20 ROM image. Its MD5 checksum is 0a59a5ba15fe8557b5f7fee32bbd393a
   138                          ;
   139                          ; To explain some perhaps non-obvious points of the ACME assembler syntax:
   140                          ;
   141                          ;     * When a branch uses the '+' symbol, the destination is the next     '+' label.
   142                          ;     * When a branch uses the '-' symbol, the destination is the previous '-' label.
   143                          ;     * Symbols '++' and '--' work similarly.
   144                          ;     * !word outputs a 16 bit value as two bytes in little endian order
   145                          ;     * !be16 outputs a 16 bit value as two bytes in big endian order
   146                          ;     * ">.address" means the high byte of .address
   147                          ;     * "<.address" means the low byte of .address
   148                          ;
   149                          ; To download the ACME assembler, see https://github.com/meonwax/acme
   150                          ; To download this document as assembly language source, see os120_acme.a
   151                          
   152                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   153                          ;
   154                          ; The Machine Operating System (MOS)
   155                          ;
   156                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   157                          ; An operating system initialises and keeps a computer running properly. This includes
   158                          ; servicing requests from hardware in a timely manner. An OS also provides a standardised
   159                          ; set of useful functionality available to user programs.
   160                          ;
   161                          ; The 6502 can directly address up to 64k of memory which is broadly assigned as follows:
   162                          ;
   163                          ;    $0000-$7FFF (32k) = RAM        (or $0000-$3FFF (16k) = RAM for Model A computer)
   164                          ;    $8000-$BFFF (16k) = BASIC or other Paged ROM (aka "Sideways ROM")
   165                          ;    $C000-$FFFF (16k) = Operating System ROM
   166                          ;
   167                          ; Each 256 bytes of memory is called a 'page', so addresses $0000 to $00FF is called 'page
   168                          ; zero', $0100-$01FF is 'page one', etc. Page zero is also known as 'zero page'.
   169                          ;
   170                          ; See circuit_mos.png
   171                          
   172                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   173                          ;
   174                          ; Memory Mapped IO ($FC00-$FEFF)
   175                          ;
   176                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   177                          ; Addresses in the range $FC00-$FEFF are not mapped to the OS ROM as might be expected, but
   178                          ; are connected to hardware devices. Consequently the CPU can communicate with hardware
   179                          ; devices by reading and writing to specific addresses in this range.
   180                          ;
   181                          ; See Chapter 3.
   182                          
   183                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   184                          ;
   185                          ; The Internal Hardware We Talk Directly To (via SHEILA)
   186                          ;
   187                          ; We use an area of memory mapped I/O called SHEILA ($FE00-$FEFF) to talk to hardware devices
   188                          ; inside the computer:
   189                          ;
   190                          ; Chip              What it does
   191                          ; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   192                          ; CRTC 6845         Cathode Ray Tube Controller. Handles the video screen format and the
   193                          ;                   cursor height.
   194                          ; ACIA 6850         } The ACIA and Serial ULA together control the RS-423 and cassette,
   195                          ; Serial ULA        } including serial printers connected to the RS-423 port.
   196                          ; Video ULA         Controls video output: timing, logical and physical colours, cursor width.
   197                          ; System VIA 6522   Controls Sound, Speech, Keyboard, and selects one of four screen hardware
   198                          ;                   addresses for scrolling purposes
   199                          ; User VIA 6522     Parallel Printer (Port A) and User Port (Port B).
   200                          ; ADC 7002          Analogue to Digital Convertor. Analogue input, often for Joysticks.
   201                          ;
   202                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   203                          
   204                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   205                          ;
   206                          ; Power On / Reset
   207                          ;
   208                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   209                          ; When powered on or reset (via the BREAK key) the 6502 executes code at the address
   210                          ; retrieved from $FFFC/D (See .resetEntryPoint).
   211                          ; The 'BREAK' key causes a soft reset. 'CTRL BREAK' causes a hard reset. A hard reset
   212                          ; clears RAM and resets more of the OS variables.
   213                          
   214                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   215                          ;
   216                          ; Interrupts
   217                          ;
   218                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   219                          ; The BBC Micro is a single processor with no threads or job queues. It uses interrupts
   220                          ; to run code concurrently with regular code. Interrupt code must finish executing in a
   221                          ; short amount of time to allow the system to continue updating effectively. Acorn
   222                          ; recommends it should last less than 2ms (4000 clock cycles).
   223                          ;
   224                          ; The CPU receives interrupts generated by hardware devices when they need attention.
   225                          ;
   226                          ; There are three interrupt types (NMI; IRQ1; IRQ2)
   227                          ;
   228                          ; NMI = non-maskable interrupts
   229                          ;   These are the highest priority of interrupt. They cannot be disabled. They are only
   230                          ;   produced by certain additional hardware (hard disk controllers and Econet).
   231                          ;   When an NMI is generated the 6502 interrupts its normal operation and executes from
   232                          ;   the location given in the OS at $FFFA-B (i.e. $0D00 = .nmiEntryPoint). This is the
   233                          ;   NMI handling code. By default this just returns immediately by executing an RTI
   234                          ;   instruction.
   235                          ;
   236                          ; IRQ1 = interrupt request 1
   237                          ;   This occurs when hardware generates an IRQ, or a BRK instruction occurs. The OS uses
   238                          ;   this IRQ extensively. If a BRK instruction was the cause then the BRKV vector is used
   239                          ;   to handle it (the OS uses this to output error messages). Otherwise the operating
   240                          ;   system checks each hardware in turn to see what needs attention. The OS services the
   241                          ;   RS-423, cassette, vertical sync, 100Hz timer, interval timer, ADC conversion (e.g. used
   242                          ;   for analogue joysticks), keyboard, speech (if speech hardware is present), and printer
   243                          ;   as needed. Based on the 100Hz timers, it also updates sounds and updates the flashing
   244                          ;   colours.
   245                          ;
   246                          ;   Finally any unhandled interrupt gets passed on to IRQ2.
   247                          ;
   248                          ; IRQ2 = interrupt request 2
   249                          ;   This is available for user code to intercept unhandled interrupts. It is the lowest
   250                          ;   priority of interrupt.
   251                          ;
   252                          ; Calls to IRQ1 and IRQ2 handlers are indirected through 'vectors' (2 byte addresses
   253                          ; stored in RAM at $0200 upwards), meaning the user can intercept them and do their own
   254                          ; processing (often then passing through any unwanted interrupts back into the original
   255                          ; default interrupt routine). NMIs cannot be redirected.
   256                          
   257                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   258                          ;
   259                          ; BRK instruction
   260                          ;
   261                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   262                          ; The BRK instruction is used for error handling. It pushes the program counter and flags
   263                          ; onto the stack (including a set bit for the BRK flag) and starts executing from the address
   264                          ; stored at $FFFE/F (which is set to .irqEntryPoint).
   265                          ;
   266                          ; The OS uses BRK to display error messages (as does the BASIC ROM). The BRK instruction
   267                          ; is followed by an error number then the error string to display. The .brkHandler code
   268                          ; displays the error message then enters the current language. Typically though, as soon
   269                          ; as the machine has started up it will be running a language such as BASIC. BASIC installs
   270                          ; its own BRK handler, at which point the OS BRK handler is no longer used.
   271                          ;
   272                          ; Note that the BRK instruction is unrelated to the BREAK key. Pressing BREAK causes the 6502
   273                          ; to RESET.
   274                          
   275                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   276                          ;
   277                          ; Paged ROMs (aka "Sideways" ROMs)
   278                          ;
   279                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   280                          ; Functionality of the BBC Micro can be extended via the use of Paged ROMs. These ROMs
   281                          ; can be quickly switched ('paged in') into the memory map as needed at address range
   282                          ; $8000-$BFFF. Once the ROM code has finished executing the ROM is 'paged out' and the
   283                          ; previous ROM reinstated into the memory map (which is normally the current language, often
   284                          ; BASIC).
   285                          ;
   286                          ; Paged ROMs can implement programming languages, provide more commands (star commands) to
   287                          ; the user, or even act as a Read Only filing system (using ROMFS).
   288                          ;
   289                          ; An optional speech ROM is also supported, which generates speech from text. It is used
   290                          ; alongside a Phrase ROM or 'PHROM' that provides the word and phoneme data for generating
   291                          ; speech.
   292                          ;
   293                          ; The BBC Micro has 5 ROM sockets available for Paged ROMs (and the MOS supports up to 16 with
   294                          ; some additional hardware). In a default configuration (as shipped) the first two ROM sockets
   295                          ; hold the OS and BASIC.
   296                          ;
   297                          ; 'Sideways RAM'
   298                          ; Later in the BBC Micro's life it was discovered that you could add 'Sideways RAM' instead
   299                          ; of a ROM. This requires a write signal as the BBC Micro is hard-wired to prevent writing to
   300                          ; the Sideways area.
   301                          ;
   302                          ; A common 'Sideways RAM' implementation consists of an expansion board (aka 'daughter board')
   303                          ; that plugs into a ROM socket with a flying lead connected to the motherboard. This can
   304                          ; then be used as either additional program RAM or (perhaps more often) to hold a ROM image.
   305                          
   306                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   307                          ;
   308                          ; Calling the Operating System
   309                          ;
   310                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   311                          ; There is a standard set of specific addresses (high in memory, between $FFB9 and $FFF7)
   312                          ; that the user calls to access OS routines. These are detailed below.
   313                          ;
   314                          ; The following entry points are the API for user programs, or other ROMs, which want to
   315                          ; access MOS facilities. Note that they occur at addresses which are guaranteed not to
   316                          ; change: for example, in all versions of the MOS, OSBYTE is at $FFF4. It is therefore safe
   317                          ; for a user program to 'JSR .OSBYTE' without knowing which MOS version is present. No other
   318                          ; addresses in the ROM are safe to jump to from outside the ROM.
   319                          ;
   320                          ; Many of these calls are then indirected through a set of 'standard vectors' located in RAM
   321                          ; at $0200-$0235. A vector is a two byte address. By default each vector contains the address
   322                          ; within the OS ROM of the relevant routine. Advanced users can change a vector to point to
   323                          ; their own routine in RAM to handle the call. This allows the user to extend or replace the
   324                          ; existing functionality provided by the OS.
   325                          ;
   326                          ; If a Paged ROM wishes to change the vectors (for example a filing system ROM would want to
   327                          ; redirect filing system calls to its own routines within its ROM) then a different system of
   328                          ; overriding these vectors is used. See the 'Extended Vectors' section below.
   329                          ;
   330                          ; Incidentally the 6502 based Commodore computers from the Commodore PET, the VIC 20 through
   331                          ; to the Commodore 64 have a similar arrangement of vectors, with its KERNAL functions.
   332                          ; See https://www.pagetable.com/?p=926 .
   333                          
   334                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   335                          ;
   336                          ; OSWRCH (For displaying text and graphics) ($FFEE)
   337                          ;
   338                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   339                          ; OSWRCH is generally used to output text or graphics. Output is usually sent to the screen,
   340                          ; but it can be redirected to other output devices, such as a printer or a file.
   341                          ;
   342                          ; OSWRCH is the equivalent of the 'VDU' command from BASIC, which we use here for brevity.
   343                          ;
   344                          ; VDU 32-126 shows ASCII text characters on screen.
   345                          ; VDU 127 is delete, which removes the previous character.
   346                          ; VDU 128-255 displays custom characters on screen (or Teletext codes in MODE 7).
   347                          ; VDU 0-31 have individual special meanings, and often require more VDU calls that act as
   348                          ; parameters. For example VDU 22 is the code to change display MODE. Calling this does
   349                          ; nothing on it's own, as it's waiting for the next VDU call (e.g. VDU 2) specifying the
   350                          ; MODE number to change to.
   351                          ;
   352                          ; Complex code is found within VDU 25 (the equivalent of BASIC's PLOT command) and is used
   353                          ; to plot points, lines, dotted lines, filled triangles, and perform horizontal line fills.
   354                          ; See Chapter 8.
   355                          ;
   356                          ; Because OSWRCH does so much, a large chuck (37%) of the operating system is devoted to it.
   357                          ;
   358                          ; See .oswrchEntryPoint.
   359                          ;
   360                          ; The BBC Micro defines eight screen MODEs (0-7) each with different combinations of pixel
   361                          ; resolutions, colours, text vs graphics capability, and therefore memory usage.
   362                          
   363                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   364                          ;
   365                          ; OSRDCH ($FFE0)
   366                          ;
   367                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   368                          ; Reads a character from the keyboard (or other input source such as RS-423 or file).
   369                          ; See .osrdchEntryPoint.
   370                          
   371                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   372                          ;
   373                          ; OSBYTE ($FFF4) (aka *FX)
   374                          ;
   375                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   376                          ; OSBYTE provides a wide range of miscellaneous functionality. This can be accessed from
   377                          ; BASIC as a '*FX' command. For example:
   378                          ;
   379                          ;   *FX 12,2
   380                          ;
   381                          ; sets the keyboard auto-repeat rate to a faster value than the default. This is equivalent
   382                          ; to the assembler version:
   383                          ;
   384                          ;   LDA #12
   385                          ;   LDX #2
   386                          ;   JSR .OSBYTE
   387                          ;
   388                          ; The A,X,Y registers are the parameters to this call to the OS. Register 'A' determines
   389                          ; the type of the call, and X and Y are the parameters for that type.
   390                          ; See .osbyteEntryPoint.
   391                          
   392                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   393                          ;
   394                          ; OSWORD ($FFF1)
   395                          ;
   396                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   397                          ; Some calls to the OS needs more parameters than an OSBYTE can provide. In this case OSWORD
   398                          ; is used. Registers X and Y together form an address that holds a parameter block of data
   399                          ; specific to the type of OSWORD given by register 'A'.
   400                          ;
   401                          ; OSWORD calls are available to machine code programs but there is no equivalent star
   402                          ; command like *FX.
   403                          ; See .oswordEntryPoint.
   404                          ; See .osbyteAndOSWORDRoutineTable (end of table for OSWORD entry points)
   405                          
   406                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   407                          ;
   408                          ; Filing System ($FFCE - $FFDD)
   409                          ;
   410                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   411                          ; A filing system is selected via a star command. The OS ships with support for tape (*TAPE)
   412                          ; and ROM filing systems (*ROM). Support for other filing systems (e.g. *ADFS, the
   413                          ; Advanced Disk Filing System) can be added using Paged ROMs. There is a range of OS calls
   414                          ; designed to perform operations on the current filing system:
   415                          ;
   416                          ;           OSFIND ($FFCE) - Open or close a file (See .osfindEntryPoint)
   417                          ;           OSGBPB ($FFD1) - Read / write multiple bytes to an open file. This is not
   418                          ;                            implemented in this OS for the TAPE/ROM filing systems.
   419                          ;                            Filing System Paged ROMs implement this.
   420                          ;           OSBPUT ($FFD4) - Write a single byte to an open file (See .osbputEntryPoint)
   421                          ;           OSBGET ($FFD7) - Read a single byte to an open file (See .osbgetEntryPoint)
   422                          ;           OSARGS ($FFDA) - Read / write a file's attributes (See .osargsEntryPoint)
   423                          ;           OSFILE ($FFDD) - Load / Save an entire file (See .osfileEntryPoint)
   424                          
   425                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   426                          ;
   427                          ; OSCLI (aka 'star commands') ($FFF7)
   428                          ;
   429                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   430                          ; The OS is shipped with a Command Line Interpreter (CLI). This is a primitive form of
   431                          ; shell. Commands can be accessed from BASIC or via the OSCLI routine.
   432                          ;
   433                          ; BASIC prefixes these with an asterisk, e.g. '*FX' etc. Hence these commands are known as
   434                          ; 'star commands'. OSCLI executes a command supplied as a string. The OS provides a standard
   435                          ; set, and Paged ROMs can add more.
   436                          ; See .oscliEntryPoint.
   437                          ; See .starCommandTable.
   438                          ;
   439                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   440                          ;
   441                          ; Others
   442                          ;
   443                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   444                          ; OSRDRM ($FFB9)                  - Read byte from Paged ROM (new in MOS 1.20)
   445                          ;                                   (See .osrdrmEntryPoint)
   446                          ; VDUCHR ($FFBC)                  - A cut down form of OSWRCH just for writing to the screen
   447                          ;                                   (See .vduChrEntryPoint)
   448                          ; OSEVEN ($FFBF)                  - Generates an EVENT, a kind of managed interrupt
   449                          ;                                   (See .eventEntryPoint)
   450                          ; GSINIT ($FFC2) / GSREAD ($FFC5) - Parse a string in memory (e.g. from an OSCLI command)
   451                          ;                                   (See .gsinitEntryPoint)
   452                          ; NVRDCH ($FFC8) / NVWRCH ($FFCB) - Non vectored versions of OSRDCH / OSWRCH
   453                          ; OSASCI ($FFE3)                  - Write character using OSWRCH, or write CR/LF if CR found
   454                          ;                                   (See .OSASCI)
   455                          ; OSNEWL ($FFE7)                  - Write CR/LF (See .OSNEWL)
   456                          
   457                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   458                          ;
   459                          ; Extended vectors
   460                          ;
   461                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   462                          ; Extended vectors allow Paged ROMs to intercept the standard vectors at $0200 upwards,
   463                          ; effectively getting them to point to an address *within a specified Paged ROM*.
   464                          ; A table containing 27 entries is stored at $0D9F (.extendedVectorSpace) with 3 bytes per
   465                          ; entry:
   466                          ;      the two byte address
   467                          ;      a single byte holding the ROM number
   468                          ;
   469                          ; To use an extended vector write appropriate values into these locations, then store
   470                          ; the address '$FF00+3*N' in OS vector at '$0200+2*N' (remembering the old address at
   471                          ; $0200+2*N if required).
   472                          ; See Chapter 23.
   473                          
   474                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   475                          ;
   476                          ; Memory Map for RAM
   477                          ;
   478                          ; ***************************************************************************************
   479                          ; $0000-$008F   Current language workspace
   480                          ; $0090-$009F   Econet workspace
   481                          ; $00A0-$00A7   NMI workspace       [unused by the OS, can be used by Paged ROMs]
   482                          ; $00A8-$00AF   "OS temp workspace" [unused by the OS, can be used by Paged ROMs]
   483                          ; $00B0-$00CF   Filing System workspace
   484                          ; $00D0-$00FF   VDU/OS workspace
   485                          ; $0100-$01FF   6502 stack (the stack works downwards from $01FF)
   486                          ; $0200-$0235   OS vectors
   487                          ; $0236-$02FF   OS variables
   488                          ; $0300-$037F   VDU variables
   489                          ; $0380-$03DF   Cassette Filing System workspace
   490                          ; $03E0-$03FF   Keyboard input buffer
   491                          ; $0400-$07FF   Workspace for the currently active language (e.g. BASIC)
   492                          ; $0800-$08FF   Sound workspace; Sound buffers; Printer buffer; Envelope storage
   493                          ; $0900-$09FF   Envelope/Speech/Cassette buffers
   494                          ; $0A00-$0AFF   Cassette/RS-423 input buffer
   495                          ; $0B00-$0BFF   Soft key buffer
   496                          ; $0C00-$0CFF   Soft character ('font') definitions
   497                          ; $0D00-$0D9E   NMI routine (used by Disc Controllers and Econet)
   498                          ; $0D9F-$0DEF   Expanded vector set
   499                          ; $0DF0-$0DFF   Paged ROM workspace storage locations
   500                          ; $0E00-$7FFF   Available user memory
   501                          ;
   502                          ; Available user memory starts at $0E00 for a standard TAPE based machine. At reset time,
   503                          ; Paged ROMs such as disc controllers can request more RAM pages from $0E00 upwards. This
   504                          ; will commonly raise the start of user memory to $1900. Soft character definitions (aka
   505                          ; 'font explosions') also raise the start of available memory.
   506                          ; See .osbyte20EntryPoint
   507                          ;
   508                          ; OSBYTE 131 reads the current OSHWM (OS High Water Mark), the true address of the start
   509                          ; of user memory after ROMs and font explosions have claimed memory. This often has the
   510                          ; same value as the PAGE variable in BASIC (unless PAGE is manually changed), which is
   511                          ; where BASIC programs are located.
   512                          ; See .osbyte131EntryPoint
   513                          ; See .currentOSHWM
   514                          
   515                          ; ***************************************************************************************
