  2743                          ; ***************************************************************************************
  2744                          ;
  2745                          ; Chapter 4: Character definitions and VDU tables
  2746                          ;
  2747                          ; ***************************************************************************************
  2748                          ; ***************************************************************************************
  2749                          
  2750                          ; ***************************************************************************************
  2751                          ;
  2752                          ; Character definitions
  2753                          ;
  2754                          ; See font.png
  2755                          ;
  2756                          ; This is the start of the OS, located at $C000.
  2757                          ;
  2758                          ; The shape of each printable character (ASCII codes 32-126) is defined below. They are used
  2759                          ; to draw text in all non-teletext MODEs 0-6 (see .displayACharacter). Character 127 (DELETE)
  2760                          ; is not a printable character, but is given a definition here anyway as a solid block of set
  2761                          ; pixels. It's drawn in the background colour to erase the character at the graphics cursor.
  2762                          ;
  2763                          ; Characters already drawn can be read from the screen, to retrieve the ASCII code from the
  2764                          ; pattern of pixels on screen. The OS itself uses this when in cursor editing mode (the two
  2765                          ; cursor editing with READ and WRITE cursors, as seen in the BASIC line editor and user text
  2766                          ; input in general). See .osbyte135EntryPoint.
  2767                          ;
  2768                          ; Historical note: The BBC Micro was Acorn's first computer to contain a character set in
  2769                          ; ROM, unless you count the seven-segment LED settings for the hex digits 0 to F in the
  2770                          ; tiny Acorn System 1. The System 2 used teletext graphics akin to the BBC's Mode 7, and
  2771                          ; the Atom's rather smaller set of symbols (which lacked lower-case letters) were similarly
  2772                          ; generated directly in the video circuitry rather than being plotted from symbols in ROM.
  2773                          ;
  2774                          ; ***************************************************************************************
  2775                          * = $C000
  2776                          
  2777                          .characterDefinitions
